v0.3.0-rc1 (8 oct 2026)

First release candidate of the 0.3 line. SPEAR becomes a controlled coding
agent with its own evidence, a workspace-aware context, external capabilities
and a memory of the workspace that keeps its provenance.

	•	Controlled coding execution: changes run on a standalone coding core
		with six tools -- read_file, search_files, patch, write_file,
		delete_file, terminal -- confined to the workspace and the sandbox.

	•	Final-state evidence: a change is VERIFIED only when a build or test
		ran and passed after the last change to the source; anything else is
		UNVERIFIED, and the answer says so where it claims success. A command
		sent to the background, or a Makefile that only prints its help, is no
		check. Declared build and test commands take precedence over probed
		ones; a project needs no configuration to be checked.

	•	Normative and MIXED work against a bound standard: questions are
		answered from the standard's provisions, and a change "the way the
		standard requires" is checked against a constraint set built from
		them. Compliance is reported only when the standard's own evidence
		establishes it -- otherwise NOT_DEMONSTRATED, never COMPLIANT on a
		passing build.

	•	Deterministic, workspace-aware context: rules, skills, knowledge,
		project metadata and capabilities are selected by workspace and task
		class, never by resemblance to the request, and nothing scoped to
		another project reaches a turn. Every eligible skill is offered.

	•	External capabilities: tools of MCP servers over stdio, registered per
		workspace, called through SPEAR's gateway with read/write policy. A
		small family is shown whole, a large one as an index read on demand.
		An unavailable provider is reported, not hidden.

	•	Workspace knowledge: typed, persistent, provenance-aware facts about a
		workspace, with states (proposed, active, stale, revoked), source
		binding and conflict detection. /remember records into it; what a
		model offers stays a proposal until the operator accepts it.

	•	Hardening: a file refused to one tool is refused to every tool,
		deletion and shell redirection included; a turn that repeats a
		refused operation is stopped; one model response is bounded.
		--fresh starts a session without its stored conversation.

	•	Release work: an integrated benchmark across several real workspaces
		and task classes, with objective oracles, gated this candidate.

Known limitations

	•	The model can still make ordinary coding mistakes; SPEAR reports what
		was shown, and a wrong change is caught only as far as the project's
		own validation reaches.

	•	VERIFIED means the project's build or tests passed on the final
		source, not that the change is proven correct.

	•	Compliance with a bound standard can remain NOT_DEMONSTRATED when the
		standard's evidence does not establish it.

	•	MCP support covers tools over stdio: no resources, prompts or HTTP
		transport.

	•	The coding core needs an OpenAI-compatible endpoint; the Anthropic
		backend serves normative and general questions only.

	•	A fact the operator records as knowledge is taken as given; bind it to
		a source file to have it checked.

v0.2.0 (30 sep 2026)

First release of SPEAR, and the first line with a release branch
(release/v0.2). It is the runtime as it stood when it was frozen after the
advisory-question and scope fixes, with the documentation and the visual
identity that came with it.

	•	An agent harness for evidence-grounded engineering: a specification
		bound as the authoritative source, the project's own trees indexed
		and retrieved from, and every reported claim tied to what was
		retrieved and run. Where the evidence does not support an answer,
		the answer is withheld and the reason given.

	•	Controlled modification of a tree: investigate, plan, edit, test,
		review. A question about a change stays read-only; a write lands on
		the target the request named and on the shared files it depends on,
		through edit_file and through the shell alike; commands stay inside
		the project; a change nothing verified is reported as unverified.

	•	Confined execution, fail-closed: every command runs under bubblewrap
		with its own resource limits, reads and writes are bounded to the
		declared trees, and network access is granted per mode.

	•	Several model backends behind one provider-neutral runtime: any
		OpenAI-compatible endpoint, local or through an SSH tunnel to a GPU
		host, and the Anthropic API. The inference server component, the
		container images and the fine-tuning machinery ship alongside.

	•	Every entry point prints the release it belongs to when it starts,
		"[spear v0.2.0] spear-chat --reds --auto", from the git tag
		(scripts/spearversion.sh); the documentation derives its version from
		the same helper.
